tally guaranteed  for him and i believe that if  uh russell wilson has to play under the  franchise tag maybe one year or two it  wouldn't be the worst thing in the world  but i also believe that the seattle  seahawks you know understand  that if they sign him long term at let's  just say 33 or whatever the number is  34. somebody obviously is going to  hopscotch that but they will spend less  money  if they sign him at an average number of  about 33 or 34 than they would if they  waited a couple of years to you know  sign them year to year and then  uh you know sign him for the long term  the one other point i would make mike is  i don't think john schneider the gm of  the seahawks can be cowed into anything  you know he has gotten beat up  time and again  richard sherman you know uh earl thomas  cam chancellor  he's he has really been beat up locally  in seattle over the last few years and  what has he said  nothing  absolutely nothing he just simply views  that as the cost of doing business he  doesn't whine he doesn't complain so  i mean as far as an april 15 deadline  for russell wilson i've not talked to  john schneider about it so i don't know  but john schneider probably slept pretty  soundly last night is all i can say  you know my theory on all of this is  that russell wilson does want to go year  to year when we do the calculation the  numbers get huge very quickly in 2020  the franchise tender based on his cap  number this year is 30.34 million the  next year 36-4 if they tag him for a  third time it spikes to  52.43 million if you add up the next  four years of what he would make if he  goes year to year it averages 34 million  a year so i think he wants to go year to  year and he's giving them a window to  try to change his mind that's his  leverage his leverage is hey this is  your opportunity to do the thing you  want me to do you want me to sign a  long-term deal you got until april 15 to  put an offer on the table that i'll sign  otherwise i'm going year to year and  we'll see if and when i hit the open  market and if i do you'll have a chance  to try to sign me along with anyone else  who may want to sign me here's the other  point too peter and this is where i  think mark rogers is going and you hit  the nail on the head he's got one  current nfl client and that's russell  wilson so he doesn't have all these  other relationships and issues and  tentacles to worry about he can focus on  getting the best deal for russell wilson  i think wilson is going to try to get  what others have tried and failed to get  cousins tried to get it darrell rivas  tried to get it maybe others have tried  to get it  wilson i think is going to try to get a  percentage of the salary cap as  guaranteed compensation so as the market  changes as the cap spikes with a new cba  new tv deals gambling money  legalized gambling revenues that flow to  the nfl as that pie grows and grows and  grows and the cap goes up and up and up  he's going to want to have that  protection so he always gets 17 18 19  percent of the cap wherever that number  goes i think that's what they want and  the question is will the seahawks give  it to him  it's a great question  if i were the seahawks in that  particular case at least for now i think  i'd rather go year to year but i do  think that eventually the only way that  makes sense for the seahawks is trying  to make a deal long term with a guy who  is as good a human being and a  representative of your team as you're  going to find  um mike i i would not be remotely  surprised at what you're saying because  mark rogers is a very very smart guy i  wouldn't be surprised at that at all  um and you know what i think he's right  i really think he's right if you believe  that you are the best  uh  you know the best option for the  seahawks and the seahawks know it why  wouldn't they do that and say okay we'll  adjust your salary every year to be 18  percent of the cap or pick 17 whatever  the number is  and think about this i mean they're  looking at 30.34 million devoted  exclusively to russell wilson next year  2020 under the cap and you can't play  any games reducing the cap number in the  short term unless you sign into a  long-term deal he's got the leverage the  next year 36-4 same thing you're stuck  with that and we're only three years  away from the seahawks facing the  dilemma peter of having to pay the guy  52.43 million for one year i don't know  where the salary cap's going to be then  but that that is beyond unprecedented to  think of that kind of a cap number for  one player but that's that's his  leverage his leverage is you change my  mind with a long-term contract by april  15 or i'm going to continue this path of  kirk cousins because i ain't kirk  cousins kirk cousins is a middle of the  pack guy that made 128 million over five  years i'm one of the top three or four  or five quarterbacks in the league and  i'm going to ride this thing out one  year at a time and either you or  somebody else is going to make me the  highest paid player in league history  maybe buy a wide margin  i  i just think that  at some point some way this is going to  get done  just because i know the affection the  seahawks have for wilson i know the  affection he has for them  and  uh mark rogers is not an incendiary  device  he isn't he's a reasonable guy  and you know it's easy for me to say  that i'm not negotiating with them but i  i believe that this thing is going to  get done because it makes too much sense  to not get done  you know the best thing they did the  last time around they being mark rogers  and russell wilson  he had just finished his third year  and because he wasn't a first round pick  the seahawks had to deal with it because  they were going to get into the  franchise tag dance if they didn't it  was only a four-year extension you know  we've seen guys like aaron rodgers do  these six years ago he did a six-year  contract back in 2013 he did another  five-year deal a four-year extension  when you are only in the league three  years that sets you up to really put  pressure on a team while you're entering  your prime i mean russell wilson said he  wants to play until he's 45 we're seeing  more and more of these guys play very  well into their late 30s tom brady's  playing very well at 41. he's still got  a lot of prime years to earn and russell  wilson is moving into  a formula if he chooses to follow it  year to year that could unlock the kind  of wealth that we've never seen an nfl  player  hi i'm mike tarico and thanks for  watching make sure to hit subscribe for  the latest news and highlights from nbc  sports

